---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Standards"
  description="What OELTKit means by standards-based: SCORM 1.2, SCORM 2004, cmi5/xAPI, and standalone web targets; a WCAG 2.2 AA commitment with per-component conformance; and a no-phone-home data posture."
  path="/standards/"
>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <span class="eyebrow">Standards</span>
      <h1>For whoever has to defend this choice</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        At some point IT, legal, or procurement will ask what this thing actually is. This page is
        the answer: which standards OELTKit packages target, what the accessibility commitment
        means concretely, and exactly where learner data goes (spoiler: to your LMS, and nowhere
        else).
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--rule" id="lms-interoperability">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>LMS interoperability</h2>
      <p>
        Every OELTKit course can be packaged for four targets from the same source. You pick the
        target; the content doesn't change.
      </p>
      <dl class="targets">
        <div class="targets__item">
          <dt>SCORM 1.2</dt>
          <dd>
            The lowest common denominator — and still the safest bet for older corporate LMSes. If
            you don't know what your LMS supports, it supports this.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div class="targets__item">
          <dt>SCORM 2004</dt>
          <dd>
            Richer status reporting than 1.2 (separate completion and success). Choose it when your
            LMS supports it and you care about that distinction.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div class="targets__item">
          <dt>cmi5 / xAPI</dt>
          <dd>
            The modern standard: granular statements to an LRS, courses that can live outside the
            LMS. Choose it if your platform is recent or you run an LRS.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div class="targets__item">
          <dt>Standalone web</dt>
          <dd>
            No LMS at all — the same course runs on any static host. Tracking calls no-op safely or
            log locally. Good for previews, portfolios, and public courses.
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>

      <h3 id="verified-on">Verified on</h3>
      <p>
        This list only ever contains platforms where the full conformance test suite has actually
        been run — that's a launch rule, not a disclaimer.
      </p>
      <ul class="verified">
        <li class="verified__empty">
          No entries yet. SCORM Cloud is the first verification target; real LMSes follow as
          they're tested. Until a platform appears here, we make no claim about it.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--rule" id="accessibility">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>Accessibility</h2>
      <p>
        OELTKit components are built to <strong>WCAG 2.2 AA</strong> — keyboard operability, visible
        focus, screen-reader tested behavior, and reduced-motion support are construction
        requirements, not an audit afterthought. The validator checks the content layer (the part
        your AI writes) so generated courses don't undo that work.
      </p>

      <h3>Per-component conformance</h3>
      <div class="todo-note" role="note">
        <p>
          <strong>TODO:</strong> the per-component conformance table is auto-generated from test
          results in <a href="https://github.com/oeltkit/oeltkit">oeltkit/oeltkit</a> and lands
          here with the first component release — it is not hand-maintained, by design.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h3>Regulatory mapping</h3>
      <p>
        WCAG 2.2 AA conformance is the basis for <strong>Section 508</strong> (US) and
        <strong>EN 301 549</strong> (EU) compliance claims. A conformance statement template you
        can adapt for procurement paperwork ships alongside the conformance table.
      </p>
      <div class="todo-note" role="note">
        <p>
          <strong>TODO:</strong> conformance statement template download pending the first
          published test run.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--rule" id="data">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>Data</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>What's tracked:</strong> what you declare in the course manifest — progress,
          scores, completion, and (on cmi5/xAPI) the statements your components emit. Nothing else.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Where it goes:</strong> to your LMS or LRS, over the standard API for the target
          you packaged. That's the whole list.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>No phone-home:</strong> OELTKit courses make no requests to OELTKit, BCL
          Training, or any third party. No analytics, no beacons, no CDN dependencies at runtime.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .lede {
    font-size: var(--oelt-text-lg);
    color: var(--oelt-color-ink-soft);
  }

  .targets {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--oelt-space-lg);
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(18rem, 100%), 1fr));
    margin: var(--oelt-space-xl) 0;
  }

  .targets__item {
    background: var(--oelt-color-surface);
    border: var(--oelt-border-w) solid var(--oelt-color-line);
    border-radius: var(--oelt-radius-md);
    padding: var(--oelt-space-lg);
  }

  .targets dt {
    font-family: var(--oelt-font-display);
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: var(--oelt-text-lg);
    margin-bottom: var(--oelt-space-xs);
  }

  .targets dd {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: var(--oelt-text-sm);
    color: var(--oelt-color-ink-soft);
  }

  .verified {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
  }

  .verified__empty {
    border: var(--oelt-border-w-bold) dashed var(--oelt-color-line-strong);
    border-radius: var(--oelt-radius-md);
    padding: var(--oelt-space-lg);
    color: var(--oelt-color-ink-soft);
    max-width: var(--oelt-measure);
  }

  .todo-note {
    max-width: var(--oelt-measure);
    margin-block: var(--oelt-space-md);
  }
</style>
